## DESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Sampling distributions)
## DBsection(Sample mean)
## Institution(The College of Idaho)
## Author(R Cruz, L Danielson, J Guild)
## Level(3)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('The Basic Practice of Statistics')
## AuthorText1('David Moore')
## EditionText1('5e')
## Section1('11')
## KEYWORDS('statistic', 'parameter', 'sampling distribution', 'sample')

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',  'PGML.pl',
    'answerHints.pl', 'PGstatisticsmacros.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

#  Setup

$mu      = random(530, 560, 0.1);
$sigma   = random(25,  30,  0.1);
$n       = random(25,  35,  5);
$sigmaSD = $sigma / sqrt($n);
$x       = int($mu) + random(4, 7, 1);

$zscore1 = Round(($x - $mu) / $sigma, 2);
$pval1   = uprob(abs($zscore1));

$zscore2 = Round(($x - $mu) / $sigmaSD, 2);
$pval2   = uprob(abs($zscore2));

# the following are the answers with additional messages for students.

$ans_a = Compute($pval1)->with(tolerance => 0.01, tolType => 'absolute');
$cmp_a = $ans_a->cmp->withPostFilter(AnswerHints(
    sub {
        my ($correct, $student, $anshash) = @_;
        return abs($student - $correct) < .002;
    } => ["Your answer is close.  Try more accuracy in your calculation."]
));

$ans_b1 = Compute($mu)->with(tolerance => 0.01, tolType => 'absolute');
$cmp_b1 = $ans_b1->cmp->withPostFilter(AnswerHints(
    sub {
        my ($correct, $student, $anshash) = @_;
        return abs($student - $correct) > .000001;
    } => ["Consider the Central Limit Theorem."]
));

$ans_b2 = Compute($sigmaSD)->with(tolerance => 0.01, tolType => 'absolute');
$cmp_b2 = $ans_b2->cmp->withPostFilter(AnswerHints(
    sub {
        my ($correct, $student, $anshash) = @_;
        return abs($student - $correct) < .005;
    } => ["Your answer is close.  Try more accuracy in your calculation."]
));

$ans_c = Compute($zscore2)->with(tolerance => 0.01, tolType => 'absolute');
$cmp_c = $ans_c->cmp->withPostFilter(AnswerHints(
    sub {
        my ($correct, $student, $anshash) = @_;
        return abs($student - $correct) < .04;
    } => ["Your answer is close.  Try more accuracy in your calculation."]
));

$ans_d = Compute($pval2)->with(tolerance => 0.05, tolType => 'absolute');
$cmp_d = $ans_d->cmp->withPostFilter(AnswerHints(
    sub {
        my ($correct, $student, $anshash) = @_;
        return abs($student - $correct) < .02;
    } => ["Your answer is close.  Try more accuracy in your calculation."]
));

BEGIN_PGML
The scores of students on the SAT college entrance examinations at a certain 
high school had a normal distribution with mean [` \mu = [$mu] `] and standard
deviation [` \sigma = [$sigma] `].  

*For parts (b) through (d), consider a simple random sample (SRS) of [$n] students who took the test.*

a)  What is the probability that a single student randomly chosen from all those taking
the test scores [$x] or higher?

    ANSWER: [_]{$cmp_a}{10}

b)  What are the mean and standard deviation of the sample mean score [` \bar x`],
of [$n] students?

    - The mean of the sampling distribution for [` \bar x `] is: [_]{$cmp_b1}{10}
    - The standard deviation of the sampling distribution for [` \bar x `] is: 
    [_]{$cmp_b2}{10}

c)  What z-score corresponds to the mean score [` \bar x`] of [$x]?

    ANSWER: [_]{$cmp_c}{10}

d)  What is the probability that the mean score [` \bar x`] of these students is [$x] or higher?

    ANSWER: [_]{$cmp_d}{10}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) This uses the normal distribution to find [`P(X>[$x])`]. Using a normal table 
    or software, this result is [$ans_a].

b) Recall that the mean of the sampling distribution is the same as the population
    and the standard deviation is [`` \frac{\mu}{\sqrt{n}}``].  Therefore 
    - mean: [$ans_b1]
    - standard deviation: [$ans_b2]
c) In this case, we use a normal distribution with mean [`\mu=[$mu]`] and
    standard deviation 
    [``` \sigma_s = \frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}} = \frac{[$sd]}{\sqrt{[$n]}} = [$sigmaSD] ```]
    The [`z`]-score is 
    [``` z = \frac{x - \mu}{\sigma_s} = \frac{[$x]-[$mu]}{[$sigmaSD]} = [$ans_c] ```]
d)  Using the [`z`]-score in in part c)
    [``` P(\bar{X}> [$x]) = P(Z > [$ans_c]) = [$ans_d].```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();       # This should be the last executable line in the problem.